Step 1: Create lab directory

On your Ubuntu WSL:

mkdir jinja2-lab
cd jinja2-lab

Create the templates directory:

mkdir templates

Your structure:

jinja2-lab/
├── hostname.yml
└── templates/
    └── test.j2

Step 2: Create the Jinja2 template

Run:

nano templates/test.j2

Put:

My hostname is {{ ansible_facts['hostname'] }}

Save:

Ctrl + O
Enter
Ctrl + X

Step 3: Create playbook

Run:

nano hostname.yml

Put:

---
- name: Write hostname
  hosts: all

  tasks:
    - name: Create hostname file
      ansible.builtin.template:
        src: templates/test.j2
        dest: /tmp/hostname

Step 4: Understand the playbook

This:

ansible.builtin.template:

means:

Use Ansible's template module.

This:

src: templates/test.j2

means:

Take this Jinja2 template from my Ansible control machine.

This:

dest: /tmp/hostname

means:

Create /tmp/hostname on the target server.

So:

WSL
 |
 | templates/test.j2
 |
 ↓
Ansible processes {{ ansible_facts['hostname'] }}
 |
 ↓
Target server
 |
 └── /tmp/hostname

This:

dest: /tmp/hostname

means:

Create /tmp/hostname on the target server.

So:

WSL
 |
 | templates/test.j2
 |
 ↓
Ansible processes {{ ansible_facts['hostname'] }}
 |
 ↓
Target server
 |
 └── /tmp/hostname

Step 6: Check the result

SSH into your server:

ssh ubuntu@SERVER_IP

Then:

cat /tmp/hostname

You might get:

My hostname is ip-172-31-5-139

On another server:

My hostname is ip-172-31-8-220

=> What happened?

Your template contained:

My hostname is {{ ansible_facts['hostname'] }}

Ansible automatically replaced:

{{ ansible_facts['hostname'] }}

with the actual hostname.

